module tools.source_snapshot_handoff

// THE WET DRIVER FOR THE SOURCE-SNAPSHOT HANDOFF, AND WHAT IT IS EVIDENCE OF.
//
// gunbc.fabric_source_snapshot is a join between two closed things -- std.fabric_storage's
// content-addressed objects and a source tree -- and a join is exactly the shape that typechecks
// while establishing nothing. DESIGN section 5 names the trap: a typecheck and a grep are not
// consumers. So the subject here is a REQUEST that runs, and the question each scenario answers is
// whether the executor received the source that was intended.
//
// WHAT MAKES IT A REAL REQUEST RATHER THAN A DEMONSTRATION. The argv is not authored here. It is
// gunbc.roadmap_execution_contract validation_operation_command over a GunbcClaimValidation, bound
// to the same PinnedPublicPlusLocalRoot layout that contract already spells for a pinned public
// checkout beside a local root -- so the words this driver hands a child process are the words the
// dispatch path hands a worker, derived from one authority. If that spelling changes, this
// instrument changes with it, because it never carried a copy.
//
// AND THE CHILD IS A CHILD. The request crosses a real process boundary through gunbc.WitnessBin.Run
// with its workdir set to the materialized destination, so the compiler that answers it resolves
// every module from the snapshot's roots and from nothing this driver holds. An in-process
// composition would pass while carrying state a real second invocation would have had to recover.
//
// WHAT IS NOT CLAIMED, stated because the neighbouring claim is the tempting one. This is one
// executor on ONE HOST reading a store on that host's disk. It establishes that the bytes travel by
// content identity and that the caller's repository is never read or written -- which is what makes
// the route serviceable across a boundary -- and it does NOT establish that a remote executor works,
// because no remote one ran. The fabric's transport is gunbc.fabric_storage_client's served store,
// and binding this route to it is the next step, not this one.

// THE INSTRUMENT IS NAMED RATHER THAN ITS OUTPUT TRANSCRIBED (DESIGN section 6). Re-derive every
// figure this module produces with:
//
//   CTRL_BUILD_MODE=local gunbc run --source-root dag --source-root src/v2 \
//     --entry dag/gunbc/instruments/source_snapshot_handoff.dag --function source_snapshot_handoff
//
// CTRL_BUILD_MODE IS PART OF THE COMMAND AND NOT ENVIRONMENT TRIVIA, for the reason
// gunbc.scm.scm_wet_execution_receipt records about its own driver: this instrument BUILDS the
// binary it spends, PATH cargo in a session container is a shim that dispatches to a remote
// amd64 builder, and an unforced run produces a binary this host cannot execute. Measured here:
// the first attempt refused with `build refused for gunbc: ctrl-build: dispatching remote build
// via BuildBuddy`, which is the located refusal doing its job rather than a flake.
import extdeps.shell
